sentences.t:
Díxoles a todos commo querié trasnochar;
vassallos tan buenos por coraçón lo an,
mandado de so señor todo lo han a far.

sentences.l:
He-told-them to all how he-wished to-march-the-night;
vassals so good by heart it they-have,
command of their lord all it they-have to do.

sentences.t:
Ante que anochesca pienssan de cavalgar;
por tal lo faze mio Çid que no [lo] ventasse nadi.
Andidieron de noch, que vagar non se dan.

sentences.l:
Before that it-should-grow-dark they-think of riding;
for such it does my Çid that not [it] should-scent nobody.
They-went by night, for rest not themselves they-give.

sentences.t:
Vos con çiento de aquesta nuestra conpaña,
pues que a Castejón sacaremos a çelada,
en él fincaredes teniendo a la çaga;
a mí dedes dozientos pora ir en algara;
con Dios e vuestra auze feremos grand ganançia.”

sentences.l:
You with hundred of this our company,
since that Castejón we-shall-draw-out by ambush,
in it you-will-remain holding at the rear;
to me may-you-give two-hundred for to-go in raid;
with God and your fortune we-shall-make great gain.”

sentences.t:
Si cueta vos f[o]re alguna al algara,
fazedme mandado muy privado a la çaga;
D’aqueste acorro fablará toda España.”

sentences.l:
If trouble to-you should-be any at-the raid,
make-me message very quickly to the rear;
Of this rescue will-speak all Spain.”

sentences.t:
Todos son exidos, las puertas abiertas an dexadas
con pocas de gentes que en Castejón fincar[a]n;
las yentes de fuera todas son derramadas.

sentences.l:
All are gone-out, the gates open they-have left
with few of people who in Castejón remained;
the peoples outside all are scattered.

sentences.t:
Mio Çid don Rodrigo a la puerta adeliñava;
los que la tienen, quando vi[d]ieron la rebata,
ovieron miedo e f[o] dese[n]parada.

sentences.l:
My Çid don Rodrigo to the gate was-making-straight;
those who it hold, when they-saw the alarm,
they-had fear and it-was abandoned.

sentences.t:
Afevos los dozientos [e] tres en el algara,
e sin dubda corren, [toda la tierra preavan];
fasta Alcalá llegó la seña de Minaya;
e desí arriba tórnanse con la ganançia,
Fenares arriba e por Guadalfajara.

sentences.l:
Behold-you the two-hundred [and] three in the raid,
and without fear they-run, [all the land they-were-plundering];
as-far-as Alcalá reached the banner of Minaya;
and thence up they-turn-themselves with the gain,
Fenares up and by Guadalfajara.
